Lesson 24: Promise aur async/await in TypeScript 🚀
Ye topic bahut important hai, kyunki real projects mein API se data lana, database operations aur network requests handle karna padta hai.
1. Synchronous vs Asynchronous
Synchronous: Code ek ke baad ek execute hota hai.
console.log("First");
console.log("Second");
console.log("Third");


Output:
First
Second
Third


Asynchronous: Kuch operations complete hone mein time lete hain. JavaScript unke complete hone ka wait kiye bina doosra code chala sakti hai.
2. Promise kya hai?
Promise ek aisa object hai jo future mein milne wale result ko represent karta hai.
Promise ki 3 states hoti hain:
- pending — result abhi nahi aaya.
- fulfilled — operation successful hua.
- rejected — operation fail hua.
const result: Promise<string> = new Promise((resolve) => {
  resolve("Data received");
});

result.then((data) => {
  console.log(data);
});


Output:
Data received


Yahan Promise<string> ka matlab hai ki successful result string hoga.
3. async/await kya hai?
async/await asynchronous code ko readable banata hai.
function getUser(): Promise<string> {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    resolve("Rahul");
  });
}

async function showUser(): Promise<void> {
  const name = await getUser();
  console.log(name);
}

showUser();


Output:
Rahul


Samjho:
- async function ko Promise-returning function banata hai.
- await Promise ke result ka wait karta hai us async function ke andar.
- Promise<void> ka matlab hai ki function koi meaningful result return nahi karta.
4. Error handling with try/catch
Real applications mein API fail bhi ho sakti hai.
async function getData(): Promise<string> {
  throw new Error("Server error");
}

async function main(): Promise<void> {
  try {
    const data = await getData();
    console.log(data);
  } catch (error: unknown) {
    if (error instanceof Error) {
      console.log(error.message);
    }
  }
}

main();


Output:
Server error


catch mein error: unknown rakhna safe hai, kyunki har thrown value zaroori nahi ki Error object ho.
5. Real-world example: API se data fetch karna
type User = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
};

async function getUsers(): Promise<User[]> {
  const response = await fetch(
    "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users"
  );

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error("API request failed");
  }

  const users: User[] = await response.json();
  return users;
}

async function main(): Promise<void> {
  try {
    const users = await getUsers();
    console.log(users);
  } catch (error: unknown) {
    console.log("Users load nahi hue");
  }
}

main();


Important: response.json() ka returned data automatically runtime par User[] validate nahi karta. Ye TypeScript annotation sirf compiler ko type batati hai; external API data ke liye runtime validation alag se karni chahiye.
